  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Tuesday)\nIssued at 108 PM MST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\n19z surface analysis had loose low from south-central MT into \nwestern SD. Water vapour loop had upper trough over the western \nCONUS with distinct shortwave over the four corners area under \n90kt jet streak. Pretty spiffy day over the CWA with sunshine over\nall but the far eastern reaches of the CWA. Concern this forecast\nrevolves around the four corners shortwave.\n\nTonight/Wednesday, shortwave zips northeast, winding into a \ncompact upper low over ND as the surface low deepens under it. Low\nwill swing a cold front into the CWA Wednesday. Ahead of it, \ntight pressure gradient expected to bring gusty southwest winds \nover the higher Black Hills per 5-7mb pressure gradient, 40-50kt \nboundary layer winds, and 60-90% chance of >55mph wind gusts \ndepicted by REFS. EFI shows 60-90th percentile gusts with shift of\ntails over far southwestern SD. Deterministic Bufkit momentum \ntransfer guidance supports with 40-55kts at the top of the mixed \nlayer, favoring the northeast/east slopes of the Black Hills when \nmaximum lower tropospheric wind max slips through. Tight pressure \ngradient behind the front. Winds look very westerly which will \ngive far northwestern and southwestern SD the highest winds where \nHigh Wind Warnings have been hoisted (60-90% chance per NBM). \nRapid City proper would be spared highest afternoon winds in this \nscenario, so Wind Advisories have been posted where needed. \nSynoptic lift/QG-forcing ahead of upper low modest, but lack of \ndeep moisture return will limit precipitation potential. Much \ninitial wasted lift per 50-100mb condensation pressure deficits. \nTemperatures will be near guidance.\n\nThursday/Friday, seasonally cool temperatures expected. Next cold \nfront arrives Friday with some gusty winds and colder temperatures \nfor the weekend. Upper trough slips through with cyclonic flow aloft \nsuggesting PoPs warranted, but again, moisture will be limited. 5-\n10F below normal temperatures persist into next week.",
